ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

LOTTERY SCHEDULING (FORK ADDITIONS)

This fork introduces a simple lottery-based scheduler and a test program.

- Overview: Each RUNNABLE process has a `tickets` count; the scheduler draws
	a random winner proportionally to tickets on each scheduling round.
- Syscall: `settickets(int n)` sets the current process's ticket count (returns 0 on success, -1 on error).
- Kernel changes: see [kernel/proc.c](kernel/proc.c), [kernel/proc.h](kernel/proc.h),
	[kernel/sysproc.c](kernel/sysproc.c), [kernel/syscall.c](kernel/syscall.c),
	and [kernel/syscall.h](kernel/syscall.h).
- User API: declaration in [user/user.h](user/user.h), stub generated via [user/usys.pl](user/usys.pl).
- Test: [user/test_scheduler.c](user/test_scheduler.c) spins a process after setting tickets.

Quick try:

1. Build and boot xv6: `make qemu`
2. In the xv6 shell, run multiple processes with different tickets, e.g.:
	 - `test_scheduler 1`
	 - `test_scheduler 10`
	 - `test_scheduler 50`
3. Use `^P` (Ctrl-P) to dump process state; you’ll see `tickets` and `turns` counters.

Notes:
- The initial `init` process starts with 1 ticket.
- `settickets(n)` requires `n >= 1`.
